verdicts.reason:
The evidence is correct: 28 of 45 production packages, none shared. But once #3 lands, the per-access I/O is already gone, and the remaining gain (a few MB of asar, tens of ms at login) does not justify re-implementing persistence of the re-link token. The snippet also has a data-loss bug. Any non-ENOENT read error (EBUSY/EPERM from antivirus or the indexer at login) renames a valid config.json to .corrupt and starts from defaults. The next persist() then overwrites the file, wiping linkIdentity. It also has none of atomically's retries on Windows rename EPERM. If ever pursued:
- quarantine the file only on SyntaxError;
- on other read errors, retry and keep persistence off until a read succeeds;
- retry the rename.

